Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever a refund is paid, it goes back the way the customer paid. If the order was paid by card, the refund goes to that card. If it was paid with store credit, the refund is given as store credit.
  2. Whenever a refund amount works out to a fraction of a rupee, round it down to a whole rupee so the refund is never more than the customer paid (e.g. ₹499.60 becomes ₹499).
  3. Whenever a refund amount is worked out, base it on what the customer actually paid for the items after any discount code. Delivery fees are not part of this amount, and neither is the original price. The coupon is not restored.
  4. Whenever a customer returns an item that is not damaged, count the days since the order was delivered, treating a negative count as day 0. Up to and including day 14, refund 100% of the amount paid for the items. From day 15 through day 30, refund 50% of it (e.g. ₹1,500 paid on a ₹2,000 dress with a 25% coupon refunds ₹1,500 by day 14 and ₹750 on days 15–30). After day 30, refund nothing.
  5. Whenever a return is not for damaged goods, never refund the delivery fee, whatever day it comes back. This includes wrong or missing items and any other reason, such as poor fit.
  6. Whenever a customer reports an item arrived damaged up to and including day 90 after delivery, refund the amount paid for the items after any discount code plus the delivery fee. If the report comes more than 90 days after delivery, refund nothing.
  7. Whenever a customer cancels a paid order that has not been delivered yet, refund what they paid for the items after any discount code, not including the delivery fee, and charge no fee.
  8. Whenever an order is final sale, it gets no refund.
  9. Whenever a refund would be under ₹50, it is not paid.
  10. Whenever an order has already been refunded, any further refund request for it gets nothing more.
  11. Whenever an order earns loyalty points, it gets 1 point for every full ₹100 paid for its items after any discount code. Delivery and tax earn nothing (e.g. ₹2,000 of products minus a ₹300 coupon is ₹1,700, which earns 17 points; ₹1,850 earns 18). If the amount paid for the items is under ₹500, the order earns 0 points (e.g. ₹450 of items plus ₹100 delivery earns 0). Orders paid with store credit earn 0 points. Final-sale orders earn double points.
  12. Whenever a day's order count is shown, it includes every order placed that day, whether it was paid, later refunded, cancelled or never paid (e.g. Monday shows all 10 orders placed).
  13. Whenever daily revenue is calculated, add up the card-paid orders only: product totals after coupon discounts plus delivery fees. Leave out tax and store-credit orders, and do not subtract same-day refunds (e.g. ₹10,000 of products − ₹1,000 coupons + ₹500 delivery = ₹9,500, ignoring ₹900 tax and an ₹800 same-day refund).
  14. Whenever a day's average is shown, divide that day's revenue by that day's number of card orders only and round down to a whole rupee (e.g. ₹1,000 across 3 orders shows ₹333). Show ₹0 when the day has no card orders, including days with no orders at all.
